sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-23> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Enumerating all 4096 Turing machines with 2 states and 2 colors shows that the maximum number of steps to finish a computation grows with input length only linearly, quadratically, or exponentially, and every exponential case has another such machine computing the same function in bounded steps.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Exhaustive search of 2-state 2-color Turing machines finds three growth rates";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Undecidability and Intractability” (from p. 753), p. 762. Scope: all 4096 Turing machines with 2 states and 2 colors, under Wolfram's halting convention"
}
